  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Interior and exterior angles of convex polygons",
  "summary": "Calculate polygon angle sums, regular angles and side counts.",
  "outcomes": [
    "1.6"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g1-lesson-05"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Calculate the interior-angle sum of any convex polygon.",
    "Relate interior and exterior angles of regular polygons.",
    "Determine a polygon's number of sides from angle information."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g1-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-16-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "From triangles to any convex polygon",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Drawing diagonals from one vertex divides an n-sided convex polygon into "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "n-2\\text{ triangles}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", so its interior-angle sum is "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "(n-2)\\times180^\\circ"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". One exterior angle at each vertex always totals 360°."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "S=(n-2)180^\\circ\\qquad E=360^\\circ"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-06-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The diagram shows a regular hexagon. Lines from one vertex divide it into 6 − 2 = 4 triangles, so its interior angles total 4 × 180° = 720°. In general, an n-sided convex polygon splits into n − 2 triangles, so its interior angles total (n − 2) × 180°. The angle e is an exterior angle: the turn from one side to the next. One exterior angle at each vertex, taken in the same direction, always totals 360°, and in a regular polygon all of them are equal."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g1-lesson-06-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-06-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For a regular polygon, "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "\\text{exterior angle}=\\frac{360^\\circ}{n}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "\\text{interior angle}=180^\\circ-\\text{exterior angle}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". To recover n, divide 360° by the exterior angle. If an interior angle is given, first subtract it from 180°. The resulting side count must be a whole number at least 3."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-16-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: angles of a regular polygon",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find one exterior angle and one interior angle of a regular polygon with 20 sides."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The 20 equal exterior angles total 360°."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "360^\\circ\\div20=18^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An interior angle and the exterior angle at the same vertex lie on a straight line."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "180^\\circ-18^\\circ=162^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The interior angles total (20 − 2) × 180° = 3240°, and 3240° ÷ 20 = 162°."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-06-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: find the number of sides",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each interior angle of a regular polygon is 140°. Find the number of sides."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "First find the exterior angle."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "180^\\circ-140^\\circ=40^\\circ"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The exterior angles total 360°."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "n=360^\\circ\\div40^\\circ=9"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "(9 − 2) × 180° = 1260°, and 1260° ÷ 9 = 140°."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-06-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not divide 360° by an interior angle. Convert to the exterior angle first. The formula "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "(n-2)\\times180^\\circ"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " gives the total interior sum, not one interior angle unless you divide by n for a regular polygon."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-16-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find the interior-angle sum of an octagon, in degrees."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "1080",
        "canonical_latex": "1080",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "An octagon splits into 8 − 2 = 6 triangles, so the sum is 6 × 180° = 1080°."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g1-lesson-06-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Interior-angle sum of an n-sided polygon = "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "(n-2)\\times180^\\circ"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". Exterior angles total 360°. For a regular polygon, "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "\\text{exterior angle}=360^\\circ\\div n"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "\\text{interior angle}=180^\\circ-\\text{exterior angle}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "To find n from an interior angle, change it to the exterior angle first, then divide 360° by it; n must be a whole number of at least 3."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
